[size=6][b]EVENT HORIZON[/b][/size]
[b]Reproduce a curator's exact mod state. Verified, not hoped.[/b]

[b]A Vortex extension for building and installing collections that actually arrive the way they were built.[/b][/center]


[size=5][b]The problem[/b][/size]

Vortex's collection system is a good idea that loses state.

Mod rules go missing. FOMOD selections get dropped. File overrides reset. Load order comes out subtly different. And "the same collection" produces a different install on a different machine — [b]silently[/b], which is the part that costs you a weekend.

Nothing fails. Nothing warns you. The game just behaves differently from the curator's, and neither of you can point at why.

[b]Event Horizon attacks that from the other end.[/b] It captures every load-bearing piece of state on the curator's side into a portable, hash-verified package, then rebuilds it on the user's side through Vortex's own installer and deploy primitives. It never touches the vanilla collection codepath.


[size=5][b]How is it different from a Vortex Collection?[/b][/size]

A Vortex Collection is a [b]list of mods[/b]. It tells your machine what to fetch.

An Event Horizon package is a [b]snapshot of a working setup[/b]. It records what the curator actually had, and checks that you ended up with it.

[quote][b]Vortex Collection:[/b] "Here are the mods. Install them."
[b]Event Horizon:[/b] "Here is the exact state that worked. Rebuild it, and prove it matches."[/quote]

That difference is the whole point. It is why the package carries SHA-256 hashes for every archive, the curator's plugin order, their ESL flags, and their mod rules — and why the installer tells you when what landed on your disk is not what the curator had.


[size=5][b]What it does[/b][/size]

[b]On the curator's side[/b]
Snapshots the active profile — mod identity and archive SHA-256, enabled state, mod rules, install order, load order, per-modtype deployment manifests, and the ESL/light flag on every plugin. Packs it into a [b].ehcoll[/b] file: a self-contained ZIP with a versioned manifest. External mods that can't be linked can be bundled into the package itself.

[b]On the user's side[/b]
Resolves every mod against what you already have, downloads or extracts the rest, and installs them [b]one at a time[/b]. Then:

[list]
[*][b]Verifies what landed.[/b] Every installed archive is hashed against the manifest and reported as [b]matches[/b], [b]differs[/b], [b]damaged[/b] or [b]unknown[/b]. Telling [i]differs[/i] from [i]damaged[/i] matters: only a damaged archive is fixed by downloading it again, and only that one isn't the curator's problem.
[*][b]Applies the curator's mod rules and LOOT userlist.[/b] See the warning below — this one replaces yours.
[*][b]Pins the curator's plugin order and restores ESL flags.[/b] Plugins you have that the collection doesn't know about are integrated LOOT-style, not dumped at the bottom.
[*][b]Replays the curator's FOMOD answers.[/b] Mods with an installer come pre-filled with exactly what the curator chose — you confirm and move on, instead of guessing your way through a dozen option screens hoping you picked what they picked.
[*][b]Writes a receipt[/b], which is how it recognises an upgrade later instead of treating it as a fresh install.
[/list]

An aborted install writes no receipt, so a half-finished run never gets mistaken for a finished one.

[b]Auditing[/b]
Export a snapshot to JSON and diff it later. Diff a reference plugins.txt against your live one. Reports are browsable in-app.


[size=5][b]Linux and Steam Deck are first-class[/b][/size]

[b]This is not "it might work under Proton". It is a supported target, and real work went into making it one.[/b]

Vortex bundles 7-Zip, and 7-Zip is spawned as an external process. Under a Wine/Proton prefix that spawn fails for reasons that have nothing to do with your files — and it surfaces as [i]"7z failed to list"[/i], which reads as [b]"your collection is corrupt"[/b] when it is perfectly fine. That single misleading error is enough to make a Linux user abandon a collection that would have installed.

So Event Horizon reads its own packages with its own ZIP reader — no external process, nothing to fail in a prefix. Timeouts are platform-aware too, because a Proton prefix legitimately needs longer than Windows, and a fixed deadline turns a slow-but-healthy install into a fake hang.

[b]The whole 950+ mod collection has been installed and verified on Linux under umu/Proton, not only on Windows.[/b] If you mod on a Steam Deck or a Linux desktop, this was built for you rather than tolerated on you.


[size=5][b]⚠ Two things it will do that you should know about[/b][/size]

[b]1. It replaces your mod rules and LOOT userlist. It does not merge them.[/b]

Everything is backed up first, and the backup reaching disk is a hard interlock — the purge does not run without it.

This is deliberate. Merging the collection's rules into yours produces a rule set that exists on nobody else's machine, and it fails [i]invisibly[/i]: every file verifies, every hash matches, and the game still loads something different. Vortex stores mod rules per-mod and the LOOT userlist per-game, so this also clears rules you set for [b]other profiles of the same game[/b]. That is not recoverable from Vortex itself, which is why the backup exists.

[b]2. Nexus Premium is strongly recommended.[/b]

Without it, installing a large collection means one manual click per mod. It is not blocked, and you will be warned — but a 900-mod collection without Premium is an afternoon of clicking.


[size=5][b]Roadmap[/b][/size]

This is an [b]alpha[/b], and it is actively being worked on. What is coming:

[list]
[*][b]Applying file overrides.[/b] Captured in full today and preserved in every package — the remaining work is applying them on the user's side.
[*][b]Applying INI tweaks.[/b] Same story: recorded now, applied next.
[*][b]More UI polish.[/b] Clearer progress while a long install runs, better reporting when something needs your attention, and a calmer dashboard.
[*][b]Undo an install.[/b] Today a failed run leaves what it installed in place and re-running picks up from there. A clean rollback is wanted.
[*][b]Richer drift reporting.[/b] Show exactly what changed between the curator's setup and yours, not merely that something did.
[/list]

Requests and bug reports genuinely shape this list — see [b]Source and issues[/b] below.


[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Vortex 2.x.[/b] Built against vortex-api 2.6.0-beta.2 (React 18). It will [b]not[/b] run on Vortex 1.16.
[*][b]Nexus Premium[/b] — strongly recommended, not required.
[*][b]Windows, or Linux via Proton.[/b] Both fully supported — see above.
[/list]


[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive from this page.
[*]In Vortex, open [b]Extensions[/b] and drop the zip onto the drop zone at the bottom of the list.
[*]Restart Vortex when prompted.
[*][b]Event Horizon[/b] appears in the sidebar.
[/list]

There is nothing to configure. Open the page and it is ready.


[size=5][b]Supported games[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Fallout 4[/b]
[*][b]Skyrim Special Edition[/b]
[*][b]Fallout 3[/b]
[*][b]Fallout New Vegas[/b]
[*][b]Starfield[/b]
[/list]

Proven on a real [b]950+ mod Fallout 4 collection[/b], on Windows and on Linux under Proton.


[size=5][b]Source and issues[/b][/size]

Source, issue tracker and full technical documentation:
[url=https://github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon]github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon[/url]

Bug reports are far more useful than endorsements at this stage. If an install goes wrong, the extension can export a diagnostic report — attach that.


